    "definition": "Modal verbs modify the mood of the main verb without changing form (no -s, no -ed). 'Can' expresses general ability or informal permission. 'Could' expresses past ability or polite requests ('Could you send the documents?'). 'May' expresses formal permission or high possibility ('You may qualify'). 'Might' expresses lower possibility or tentative outcome ('The transaction might take longer').",
    "analogy": "Grado de cortesía en llamadas profesionales: 'Can you help me?' (informal) -> 'Could you please assist me?' (cortés/profesional) -> 'May I speak with the account holder?' (muy formal y respetuoso).",
    "code_example": "Caller: 'Could you verify if my doctor is in-network?'\nRepresentative: 'Yes, you may qualify for zero copay, though prior authorization might be required.'\n---\nCan: 'You can access your account online.' (Ability/Permission)\nCould: 'Could you verify your date of birth?' (Polite request)\nMay: 'May I place you on a brief hold?' (Formal permission)\nMight: 'It might take 3 to 5 business days.' (Possibility)",
    "quiz_question": "¿Cuál es la fórmula más cortés para solicitar información a un usuario en una llamada de servicio?",
